    <briefdescription>
<para>The complete monomial basis <formula id="82">$ \boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{x}) $</formula> of order <formula id="80">$ n $</formula> in <formula id="78">$ d $</formula> dimensions, as a table of exponents. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The entries are all monomials <formula id="83">$ x_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots x_d^{\alpha_d} $</formula> with <formula id="84">$ \alpha_1 + \dots + \alpha_d \le n $</formula>, ordered with the exponent of the last coordinate varying slowest and that of the first coordinate fastest. For example, in 2D with <formula id="85">$ n = 2 $</formula>: <formula id="86">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_1^2,\ x_2,\ x_1 x_2,\ x_2^2]^T $</formula>; in 3D with <formula id="87">$ n = 1 $</formula>: <formula id="88">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_2,\ x_3]^T $</formula>. The first entry is always <formula id="89">$ 1 $</formula>.</para>
<para>The exponents <formula id="90">$ \boldsymbol{\alpha} $</formula> of every entry are listed once, at construction; an evaluation is then the product of the powers, <formula id="91">$ H_k = \prod_j x_j^{\alpha_{kj}} $</formula>, and its gradient that product with the factor of coordinate <formula id="15">$ i $</formula> replaced by its derivative,  <formula id="92">$ \partial H_k / \partial x_i = \alpha_{ki}\,
x_i^{\alpha_{ki} - 1} \prod_{j \neq i} x_j^{\alpha_{kj}} $</formula>. The powers are formed by multiplication, and the factors are multiplied in the order of the coordinates. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
